WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Good to have you on Planetarian Daily, episode 77 for June 3, 2026.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.420
Some interesting findings to cover today.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.300
Engineered hookworms can now produce and deliver therapeutics inside a living host.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.520
Researchers at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.680
engineered hookworms to secrete therapeutic molecules while living inside the host gut.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.940
The parasites already survive for years by releasing molecules that allow coexistence

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.020
with human hosts. The team redirected this secretion system so the worms manufacture and release

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.580
a chosen drug. This approach exploits the worm's long-term persistence rather than relying on repeated

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.860
dosing. Next steps include testing safety and dosing control in animal models.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:52.360
Now shifting to a very different area of research. Conversion of wetlands since 1985 has added more

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.540
than 10 billion dollars to US residential flood insurance claims.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.660
Riverine flooding accounts for the measured increasing claims.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.800
The analysis matched historical wetland maps with insurance records at fine spatial scales.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.520
Wetlands reduce peak flows by storing and slowing water during storms.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.300
Future work will examine how restoration projects alter these cost trajectories.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.760
Most people assume wetlands are low-value areas that can be drained or filled with little

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.020
consequence. In reality, they actively store floodwater and reduce peak river flows during storms.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.620
Right now, as heavy rain falls on developed landscapes, the absence of those natural sponges

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.700
is increasing insurance payouts and property damage. Wetland loss since 1985 has already

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.640
added more than 10 billion dollars to US residential flood claims.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.280
The next time local planning discussions turn to unused land near rivers or coasts,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.160
the measurable cost of removing those buffers is worth tracking.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.020
On a different note, viable cold-adapted yeasts and a complex profile of gut and skin microbes

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:06.800
were recovered from Oatsy the Iceman's tissues. URAC research scientists reconstructed

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.880
the microbial profile associated with Europe's oldest natural mummy.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.380
The analysis captured gut flora, skin microbes, and cold-adapted yeasts preserved with the body.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.740
Some yeasts remained viable when cultured in the lab. Comparisons with modern samples highlight

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:29.980
shifts in microbial diversity over millennia. Additional genomic sequencing will trace lineage

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.760
continuity with modern strains. From long-term biological records preserved in ice,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.820
we move to new imaging tools that resolve molecular structures at fine scales today.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.820
A contact-free Terahertz method now maps mirror image molecular handedness at 100 micrometer

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.340
resolution across material samples. The new Terahertz technique distinguishes mirror image

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.940
molecular structures without physical contact. Chiral molecules show different behaviors

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.320
despite identical composition, a property central to drug design and biology. The method

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:13.160
achieves spatial maps at 100 micrometer resolution across material samples. It reveals handedness

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.660
patterns that conventional imaging misses at this scale. Researchers aim to extend the approach

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:24.480
to dynamic samples and thicker specimens. High-resolution molecular mapping in materials

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.600
leads us to high-resolution mapping of molecular barriers inside tumors.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.740
Spatial proteomics showed that stromal components consistently limit therapeutic

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:42.660
antibody access to solid tumor regions across multiple human cancer types. Researchers image

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:49.640
interactions between therapeutic antibodies and the tumor micro-environment using spatial proteomics.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.920
Strongable components consistently limited antibody penetration into solid tumor regions.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.660
The barriers appear conserved across multiple human tumor types examined.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.820
Mapping these physical and molecular obstacles identifies where delivery fails.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.380
The technique combines high-plex protein detection with spatial coordinates.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:17.560
Inside tumors, delivery is physically constrained. Inside bacteria, a single tool can now control

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:25.580
entire groups of genes. The sonar platform uses one engineered CAS-13 protein whose CRE-R-RNA

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:31.720
sequence can be switched to degrade, repress, or activate multiple genes in a bacterial operon.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:39.220
The sonar system uses one engineered CAS-13 protein to regulate multiple genes in an operon. Simple

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:45.400
changes to the CRE-RNA sequence switch the effector between transcript degradation, translation

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:51.320
inhibition, or activation. The approach works in living bacteria without requiring multiple distinct

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:59.360
proteins. It provides programmable RNA-level control at the operon scale. Test confirmed orthogonality

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.540
across different targets. Programmable control inside microbes brings us to questions of how well-current

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:12.440
AI tools control strain selection for flu vaccines. Two linked analyses found no consistent superiority

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:18.300
of AI predictions over standard antigenic cartography and ferret models across seasons.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.200
Two linked papers in nature medicine examine claims that artificial intelligence outperformed

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:30.540
standard methods for choosing influenza vaccine strains. Current evidence does not show consistent

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:36.580
superiority across multiple seasons or metrics. The analyses compared AI predictions against

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:43.100
historical strain selection outcomes. Authors note that larger, prospective datasets are still needed.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.800
Standard antigenic cartography and ferret models remain the regulatory baseline.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:54.340
From vaccine strain decisions that affect millions, we shift to direct observations of

00:05:54.340 --> 00:06:01.040
care quality during childbirth. In Nepal, Senegal, and Zambia, high facility birth coverage did not

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.820
translate into recommended clinical practices for monitoring, infection prevention, and newborn care.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:12.920
A nature medicine study assessed care provided by skilled health personnel in Nepal, Senegal,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:19.060
and Zambia. High coverage of facility birth did not translate into recommended clinical practices.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.140
Direct observations revealed gaps in monitoring, infection prevention, and immediate newborn care.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.400
The findings come from standardized assessments across multiple facilities in each country.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.180
Quality shortfalls persist even where access has expanded rapidly.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.660
Human health systems still show quality shortfalls. We close with how entire food systems face

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:47.400
simultaneous pressures. Climate extremes, dire related disease, biodiversity loss,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.020
and greenhouse gas emissions are all linked to current production and consumption patterns in Europe.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:58.980
A review outlines how climate-driven droughts and floods compound existing stresses on

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:05.240
European agriculture. Diet-related diseases, biodiversity loss, and greenhouse gas emissions

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.740
all trace partly to current production and consumption patterns. The system must deliver

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:16.060
affordable food while meeting climate and nature targets. Integrated approaches that address

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:22.540
production, diet, and land used together show the greatest potential. Modeling efforts will quantify

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.580
trade-offs across these goals. Keep an eye on follow-up safety tests for the engineered

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:33.720
hookworm platform in animal models. That's Planetarian Daily for Today. If you enjoyed this,

